INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','easy','mcq','Which insulin analogue has the fastest onset of action, making it suitable for mealtime dosing?','["A. Insulin lispro","B. Insulin NPH","C. Insulin glargine","D. Regular insulin"]','A','Insulin lispro (Humalog) is a rapid-acting analogue with onset 5-15 minutes, peak 30-90 minutes, and duration 3-5 hours. It was engineered by reversing the B28 Pro and B29 Lys residues to reduce self-aggregation, allowing faster subcutaneous absorption.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','A patient on insulin NPH experiences hypoglycaemia at 2 AM. Which pharmacokinetic property of NPH most likely explains this?','["A. Onset within 15 minutes","B. Peak effect occurring 4-12 hours after injection","C. Duration exceeding 24 hours","D. No identifiable peak"]','B','NPH (Neutral Protamine Hagedorn) is an intermediate-acting insulin with onset 1-2 h, peak 4-12 h, and duration 14-24 h. A late evening dose peaks in the early hours, risking nocturnal hypoglycaemia if the dose is not timed carefully relative to the evening meal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','Insulin glargine differs from human insulin primarily in that it:','["A. Has a rapid onset within 15 minutes","B. Has a pronounced peak at 6 hours","C. Provides a relatively flat, peakless 24-hour profile","D. Must be mixed with NPH before injection"]','C','Glargine forms microprecipitates at physiological pH after subcutaneous injection due to substitution of glycine at A21 and addition of two arginines at B30, providing slow, steady release with no pronounced peak and approximately 24-hour duration, mimicking basal insulin secretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','hard','mcq','A GLP-1 receptor agonist is added to a patients regimen. Which mechanism best explains its primary glucose-lowering effect?','["A. Blocking renal glucose reabsorption via SGLT-2","B. Stimulating pancreatic beta-cells in a glucose-dependent manner and suppressing glucagon","C. Closing ATP-sensitive potassium channels on beta-cells","D. Activating PPAR-gamma to improve insulin sensitivity"]','D','GLP-1 receptor agonists (e.g., liraglutide, semaglutide) bind GLP-1 receptors on pancreatic beta-cells, stimulating insulin secretion only when glucose is elevated (glucose-dependent), suppressing glucagon from alpha-cells, and slowing gastric emptying. PPAR-gamma activation is the mechanism of thiazolidinediones.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','easy','mcq','Which insulin preparation is the only one suitable for intravenous infusion in DKA?','["A. Insulin lispro","B. Insulin glargine","C. Regular (soluble) insulin","D. Insulin detemir"]','A','Regular insulin (soluble insulin) is the only insulin suitable for intravenous infusion. Its IV onset is immediate with a short duration allowing precise titration. Insulin analogues like glargine cannot be given IV. In DKA, a fixed-rate IV insulin infusion of 0.1 units/kg/hr of regular insulin is standard protocol.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','Exenatide is classified as a GLP-1 receptor agonist. Which adverse effect is most commonly reported with this class?','["A. Weight gain","B. Nausea and vomiting","C. Hypoglycaemia when used as monotherapy","D. Peripheral oedema"]','B','GLP-1 agonists slow gastric emptying and act on hypothalamic centres to reduce appetite, causing nausea and vomiting as the most common adverse effects, especially on dose initiation or escalation. They cause hypoglycaemia only in combination with insulin or sulfonylureas, not as monotherapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','Which insulin has the longest duration of action, approximately 40+ hours, allowing flexible once-daily dosing?','["A. Insulin glargine U-100","B. Insulin degludec","C. Insulin detemir","D. Insulin NPH"]','C','Insulin degludec (Tresiba) forms stable soluble multi-hexamers in subcutaneous tissue that slowly dissociate, providing a duration exceeding 40 hours. This ultra-long action allows dosing at different times each day (if needed) with lower nocturnal hypoglycaemia risk compared to glargine in some trials.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','hard','mcq','A patient starting semaglutide is counselled about a black-box warning. Which risk does this warning address?','["A. Severe hypoglycaemia in type 1 diabetes","B. Thyroid C-cell tumours (medullary thyroid carcinoma)","C. Lactic acidosis in renal impairment","D. Bladder cancer with long-term use"]','D','GLP-1 receptor agonists including semaglutide, liraglutide, and exenatide carry an FDA black-box warning for risk of thyroid C-cell tumours (medullary thyroid carcinoma) based on rodent carcinogenicity studies. They are contraindicated in patients with personal or family history of MTC or multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN2).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','easy','mcq','Insulin aspart (NovoRapid) is best described as which type of insulin?','["A. Long-acting basal insulin analogue","B. Intermediate-acting insulin","C. Rapid-acting insulin analogue","D. Short-acting regular human insulin"]','A','Insulin aspart is a rapid-acting analogue with onset 10-20 minutes, peak 1-3 hours, and duration 3-5 hours. Created by substituting B28 proline with aspartic acid to reduce self-aggregation. It is used at mealtimes (prandial insulin) in basal-bolus regimens.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Insulin & Injectable Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','Pramlintide is an antidiabetic injectable. Which statement best describes its mechanism?','["A. It is a GLP-1 receptor agonist causing incretin effects","B. It is a synthetic amylin analogue that suppresses postprandial glucagon and slows gastric emptying","C. It is an intermediate-acting insulin","D. It increases hepatic glucose production to prevent hypoglycaemia"]','B','Pramlintide is a synthetic analogue of amylin, co-secreted with insulin from beta-cells. It suppresses postprandial glucagon release, slows gastric emptying (reducing postprandial glucose excursion), and promotes satiety. It is given subcutaneously before meals as an adjunct to mealtime insulin in both type 1 and type 2 diabetes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','easy','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of metformin?','["A. Activates AMPK, reducing hepatic gluconeogenesis and increasing peripheral insulin sensitivity","B. Stimulates insulin secretion from pancreatic beta-cells","C. Blocks SGLT-2 in the renal proximal tubule","D. Activates PPAR-gamma receptors"]','A','Metformin activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK), which suppresses hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis (reducing hepatic glucose output). It also improves peripheral insulin sensitivity and may reduce intestinal glucose absorption. It does not stimulate insulin secretion and is weight-neutral to weight-reducing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','A patient with type 2 diabetes has eGFR 28 mL/min/1.73m2. Which oral antidiabetic must be stopped?','["A. Sitagliptin","B. Metformin","C. Pioglitazone","D. Repaglinide"]','B','Metformin is contraindicated when eGFR falls below 30 mL/min/1.73m2 because reduced renal clearance leads to drug accumulation and increased risk of lactic acidosis. It should be withheld when eGFR 30-45 with caution. Sitagliptin is renally cleared and requires dose reduction; pioglitazone can be continued with care in CKD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','Glibenclamide (glyburide) lowers blood glucose primarily by which mechanism?','["A. Activating AMPK in the liver","B. Inhibiting DPP-4 enzyme to increase GLP-1 levels","C. Closing ATP-sensitive K+ channels on pancreatic beta-cells, triggering insulin release","D. Blocking SGLT-2 in renal tubules to promote glycosuria"]','C','Sulfonylureas bind the SUR1 subunit of the ATP-sensitive K+ (K-ATP) channel on beta-cells, causing channel closure, membrane depolarisation, calcium influx, and insulin exocytosis. This mechanism is glucose-independent, hence the risk of hypoglycaemia even in the fasting state.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','hard','mcq','Which SGLT-2 inhibitor specifically demonstrated reduction in hospitalisation for heart failure in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial?','["A. Canagliflozin","B. Dapagliflozin","C. Ertugliflozin","D. Empagliflozin"]','D','Empagliflozin (EMPEROR-Reduced trial) significantly reduced the composite of CV death or hospitalisation for heart failure in patients with HFrEF, regardless of diabetes status. Dapagliflozin showed similar benefit in DAPA-HF. Both are now indicated for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction irrespective of T2DM.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','easy','mcq','Which class of oral antidiabetics acts by inhibiting DPP-4, thereby increasing endogenous incretin (GLP-1) levels?','["A. Thiazolidinediones","B. Biguanides","C. DPP-4 inhibitors (gliptins)","D. Sulfonylureas"]','A','DPP-4 inhibitors (gliptins: sitagliptin, saxagliptin, linagliptin, alogliptin) inhibit the enzyme that rapidly degrades GLP-1 and GIP, prolonging their action. This results in glucose-dependent insulin secretion and glucagon suppression with low hypoglycaemia risk as monotherapy. They are weight-neutral.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','Pioglitazone (a thiazolidinedione) is contraindicated in which condition?','["A. Type 2 diabetes with hypertension","B. Heart failure (NYHA class III-IV)","C. Patients with eGFR > 60","D. Patients on metformin monotherapy"]','B','Thiazolidinediones activate PPAR-gamma receptors, increasing insulin sensitivity. However, they cause sodium and water retention, precipitating or worsening heart failure. They are contraindicated in NYHA class III-IV heart failure. Additional risks include bone fractures (particularly in women) and increased bladder cancer risk with prolonged pioglitazone use.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','Which side effect is unique to SGLT-2 inhibitors and not shared with other oral antidiabetics?','["A. Weight gain","B. Lactic acidosis","C. Euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis","D. Hypoglycaemia as monotherapy"]','C','SGLT-2 inhibitors can precipitate euglycaemic DKA - ketoacidosis at near-normal blood glucose levels (typically <11 mmol/L). Mechanism: glycosuria reduces insulin secretion stimulus, raised glucagon:insulin ratio promotes lipolysis and ketogenesis. It can be missed clinically because glucose is not markedly elevated. Patients should check ketones when unwell.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','hard','mcq','A patient on metformin for 4 years develops macrocytic anaemia. Which mechanism explains this?','["A. Metformin inhibits folate absorption in the duodenum","B. Metformin impairs ileal absorption of vitamin B12 by interfering with calcium-dependent binding of the B12-intrinsic factor complex","C. Metformin causes immune haemolysis","D. Metformin suppresses erythropoietin synthesis in the kidney"]','D','Metformin reduces vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum by interfering with calcium-dependent binding of the intrinsic factor-B12 complex to ileal receptors. This is dose-dependent and occurs in 10-30% of long-term users. Annual B12 monitoring is recommended for patients on metformin for >4 years or if anaemia or neuropathy develops.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','easy','mcq','Which oral antidiabetic is associated with modest weight loss rather than weight gain?','["A. Glibenclamide","B. Pioglitazone","C. Metformin","D. Glipizide"]','A','Metformin is associated with modest weight loss (-1 to -2 kg) or weight neutrality, in contrast to sulfonylureas (weight gain ~2-4 kg) and thiazolidinediones (weight gain 2-5 kg). This is partly due to reduced appetite and GI side effects. Metformin is therefore preferred in overweight/obese type 2 diabetes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Oral Antidiabetics','medium','mcq','Acarbose reduces postprandial glucose by which mechanism?','["A. Stimulating insulin secretion from beta-cells","B. Inhibiting intestinal alpha-glucosidase, delaying carbohydrate absorption and blunting postprandial glucose spike","C. Blocking hepatic glucose output via AMPK","D. Inhibiting SGLT-2 in the kidney to cause glycosuria"]','B','Acarbose is an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor that competitively inhibits brush-border intestinal alpha-glucosidases (maltase, sucrase, glucoamylase), slowing digestion of complex carbohydrates and disaccharides. This blunts postprandial glucose spikes. Main side effects are GI: flatulence, bloating, and diarrhoea from fermentation of unabsorbed carbohydrate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','easy','mcq','Which fluid is used as first-line resuscitation in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)?','["A. 5% dextrose","B. 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline)","C. 0.45% sodium chloride","D. Hartmanns solution"]','A','0.9% normal saline is the first-line resuscitation fluid in DKA. 1 L is typically given over the first hour. The primary goal is volume resuscitation to restore circulation. 5% dextrose is added once blood glucose falls below 14 mmol/L to prevent hypoglycaemia while continuing insulin to clear ketones.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','medium','mcq','In DKA management, insulin infusion should only start after which critical check?','["A. After urine output is established and creatinine normalises","B. After confirming serum potassium is greater than 3.5 mmol/L","C. After bicarbonate infusion to correct severe acidosis","D. After blood glucose falls below 20 mmol/L"]','B','Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly via Na+/K+-ATPase stimulation. If serum K+ is already low (<3.5 mmol/L), starting insulin can cause fatal hypokalaemia and cardiac arrhythmias. Potassium must be corrected to >3.5 mmol/L before insulin infusion begins. Potassium replacement is given alongside insulin when K+ is 3.5-5.5 mmol/L.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','medium','mcq','Why must potassium be closely monitored and replaced during DKA treatment?','["A. Saline infusion dilutes serum potassium to dangerous levels","B. Insulin drives potassium intracellularly, rapidly reducing serum potassium despite total body depletion","C. Vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis and urinary potassium excretion","D. Bicarbonate therapy increases renal potassium wasting"]','C','Despite initial normal or high serum K+ (due to acidosis-driven K+ shift out of cells), total body K+ is depleted from polyuria and vomiting. Insulin therapy reverses the transcellular shift, driving K+ back into cells. Without adequate potassium replacement, serum K+ can fall precipitously, causing life-threatening arrhythmias.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','hard','mcq','An unconscious type 1 diabetic has blood glucose 1.8 mmol/L and no IV access. What is the optimal immediate treatment?','["A. Oral glucose gel squeezed between cheek and gum","B. Intramuscular glucagon 1 mg","C. Nasogastric glucose administration","D. Subcutaneous insulin withheld and await spontaneous recovery"]','D','IM glucagon 1 mg is the treatment of choice for severe hypoglycaemia when IV access is unavailable. Glucagon activates hepatic glucagon receptors, stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, rapidly raising blood glucose. Oral glucose cannot be given to unconscious patients (aspiration risk). Intranasal glucagon is an alternative where available.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','easy','mcq','What is the appropriate first-line treatment for mild hypoglycaemia in a conscious patient?','["A. 15-20 g of fast-acting carbohydrate orally, repeated after 15 minutes if glucose remains low","B. Intramuscular glucagon","C. Intravenous 50% dextrose (25g)","D. 1 mg subcutaneous glucagon"]','A','The rule of 15 applies: give 15-20 g fast-acting carbohydrate (3-4 glucose tablets, 150-200 mL fruit juice, 5 jelly babies). Recheck glucose after 15 minutes; repeat if still below 4 mmol/L. Once recovered, a starchy snack prevents recurrence. If the patient cannot swallow or is drowsy, escalate to glucagon or IV dextrose.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','medium','mcq','Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS) differs from DKA primarily in that HHS:','["A. Has lower blood glucose (typically < 20 mmol/L)","B. Is characterised by severe ketonaemia and acidosis","C. Has severe dehydration and hyperosmolality without significant ketosis or acidosis","D. Requires more rapid insulin correction"]','B','HHS is characterised by: blood glucose >30 mmol/L, plasma osmolality >320 mOsm/kg, severe dehydration (often 8-10 L deficit), and an absence of significant ketonaemia/acidosis. Residual insulin secretion prevents lipolysis. Fluid replacement is the primary treatment; insulin is introduced cautiously after 1-2 L fluid to avoid rapid osmolality falls causing cerebral oedema.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','medium','mcq','Octreotide is occasionally used adjunctively in sulfonylurea-induced refractory hypoglycaemia. Its mechanism is:','["A. Stimulating hepatic glycogenolysis via glucagon receptors","B. Inhibiting insulin secretion from pancreatic beta-cells via somatostatin receptors","C. Blocking GLUT-2 transporters in hepatocytes","D. Activating glucocorticoid receptors to increase gluconeogenesis"]','C','Octreotide (a somatostatin analogue) inhibits pancreatic insulin secretion by binding SSTR2/5 on beta-cells, which is valuable in managing prolonged or refractory hypoglycaemia from sulfonylurea overdose or insulinoma. It prevents continued insulin release driven by the drugs persistent binding to K-ATP channel SUR1 subunit.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','hard','mcq','A patient with Addisons disease presents in hypoglycaemic coma. Why is this patient particularly susceptible to hypoglycaemia?','["A. Mineralocorticoid deficiency causes excessive urinary glucose loss","B. Cortisol deficiency impairs gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, and reduces counter-regulatory response to hypoglycaemia","C. ACTH excess drives glucose into peripheral tissues","D. Aldosterone deficiency causes severe hyponatraemia that masks hypoglycaemia"]','D','Cortisol is a key counter-regulatory hormone that stimulates hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, antagonises insulin signalling in peripheral tissues, and promotes fat and protein breakdown to fuel glucose synthesis. In adrenal insufficiency, loss of cortisol severely impairs counter-regulation, allowing hypoglycaemia to develop and persist.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','easy','mcq','When restarting subcutaneous insulin after DKA resolution, what timing rule prevents rebound ketosis?','["A. Give subcutaneous insulin simultaneously with stopping IV infusion","B. Give the first subcutaneous dose 30-60 minutes before stopping the IV insulin infusion","C. Wait 2 hours after stopping IV insulin before giving subcutaneous","D. Restart subcutaneous insulin only after blood glucose rises above 15 mmol/L"]','A','To prevent gap between IV and SC insulin cover, the first SC insulin dose must be given 30-60 minutes before discontinuing IV insulin. This allows time for the SC insulin to be absorbed. Stopping IV insulin abruptly without SC cover risks rapid ketone regeneration and recurrence of DKA, even if blood glucose appears controlled.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Diabetic Emergencies','medium','mcq','Which electrolyte abnormality is the most life-threatening complication requiring correction before insulin therapy in DKA?','["A. Hyponatraemia < 130 mmol/L","B. Hypokalaemia (K+ < 3.5 mmol/L)","C. Hypophosphataemia < 0.5 mmol/L","D. Hypomagnesaemia < 0.5 mmol/L"]','B','Insulin causes potassium to shift intracellularly. If serum potassium is already below 3.5 mmol/L at the start of DKA treatment, adding insulin without first correcting K+ can cause profound hypokalaemia and potentially fatal cardiac arrhythmias (VF, VT). Potassium must be normalised to >3.5 mmol/L before insulin infusion commences.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','easy','mcq','Levothyroxine (T4) is the standard treatment for hypothyroidism. What is its primary mechanism?','["A. Synthetic T4 converted peripherally by deiodinase to active T3, which binds nuclear receptors to regulate metabolism","B. Stimulates TSH secretion from the pituitary","C. Inhibits thyroid peroxidase","D. Blocks iodine uptake by the thyroid via the sodium-iodide symporter"]','A','Levothyroxine is synthetic T4, which undergoes peripheral deiodination by type 1 deiodinase (predominantly in liver and kidney) to produce active T3. T3 binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors (THR-alpha and THR-beta) to regulate gene expression controlling metabolism, cardiac function, growth, and development.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','medium','mcq','Carbimazole treats hyperthyroidism by which primary mechanism?','["A. Inhibiting thyroid peroxidase, blocking organification of iodide into thyroglobulin","B. Preventing iodine absorption in the gut","C. Blocking TSH receptors on thyroid follicular cells","D. Promoting thyroid hormone degradation in the liver"]','B','Carbimazole (a prodrug converted to methimazole in vivo) inhibits thyroid peroxidase, the enzyme that oxidises iodide and catalyses the organification of iodide into thyroglobulin (MIT, DIT formation). This blocks synthesis of new T3 and T4 but does not affect release of pre-formed hormone, so clinical effect is delayed 3-4 weeks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','medium','mcq','Why is propylthiouracil (PTU) preferred over carbimazole specifically during the first trimester of pregnancy?','["A. PTU has shorter half-life allowing more precise dosing","B. PTU does not cross the placenta at all","C. PTU has fewer teratogenic effects; carbimazole is linked to aplasia cutis, choanal atresia, and oesophageal atresia","D. PTU stimulates fetal thyroid development"]','C','PTU is preferred in the first trimester because carbimazole and methimazole are associated with embryopathy including aplasia cutis congenita, choanal and oesophageal atresia (carbimazole embryopathy syndrome). After the first trimester, treatment is switched to carbimazole due to PTUs risk of severe hepatotoxicity. PTU also has the additional benefit of blocking peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','hard','mcq','In thyroid storm, propranolol provides rapid symptomatic control via beta-blockade. Which additional unique pharmacological action makes it particularly valuable?','["A. It stimulates iodine uptake by the thyroid to feedback-inhibit hormone synthesis","B. It inhibits peripheral type 1 deiodinase, reducing conversion of T4 to more potent T3","C. It blocks thyroid peroxidase directly","D. It promotes renal clearance of circulating T3"]','D','At high doses used in thyroid storm, propranolol not only blocks beta-adrenergic receptors (reducing tachycardia, tremor, anxiety) but also inhibits type 1 deiodinase in peripheral tissues, reducing conversion of T4 to the more potent T3. This dual action is uniquely beneficial in thyroid storm when rapid reduction of thyroid hormone effect is needed.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','easy','mcq','Radioiodine (I-131) therapy for hyperthyroidism works by which mechanism?','["A. Blocking iodine uptake by the thyroid via sodium-iodide symporter","B. Being selectively taken up by thyroid cells and emitting beta radiation that destroys them","C. Stimulating TSH-independent thyroid hormone release","D. Inhibiting thyroid peroxidase enzyme"]','A','I-131 is taken up selectively by thyroid follicular cells via the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS). The beta radiation emitted (high ionising energy, short tissue path 0.5-2 mm) selectively destroys thyroid tissue. Hypothyroidism is a common long-term consequence requiring lifelong levothyroxine replacement in up to 80% of patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','medium','mcq','High-dose iodide (Lugols solution) is given pre-thyroidectomy. The Wolff-Chaikoff effect describes:','["A. High iodide intake paradoxically inhibiting thyroid hormone synthesis and reducing gland vascularity","B. Low iodide intake stimulating TSH secretion","C. Iodide promoting thyroid gland vascularity before surgery","D. Iodide accelerating T4 to T3 conversion"]','B','The Wolff-Chaikoff effect is the paradoxical inhibition of thyroid hormone synthesis when the gland is exposed to an acute large iodide load. This temporarily blocks organification, reducing hormone production and gland vascularity. Lugols iodine given 10 days pre-operatively shrinks the gland, reduces its vascularity, and makes surgery safer and less bloody.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','medium','mcq','A patient on levothyroxine starts taking ferrous sulphate. What interaction must be managed?','["A. Iron increases levothyroxine bioavailability","B. Ferrous sulphate chelates levothyroxine in the gut reducing absorption; separate doses by at least 4 hours","C. The combination increases risk of thyroid storm","D. Levothyroxine reduces iron absorption requiring supplementation"]','C','Ferrous sulphate (and calcium carbonate, antacids, colestyramine, proton pump inhibitors) significantly reduces levothyroxine absorption by chelation in the gastrointestinal tract. Patients should be counselled to take levothyroxine on an empty stomach at least 30-60 minutes before food, and separate iron supplements by a minimum of 4 hours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','hard','mcq','Amiodarone causes thyroid dysfunction through multiple mechanisms. Which statement is accurate?','["A. Amiodarone causes only hypothyroidism via iodine loading","B. Amiodarone (37% iodine by weight) can cause both hypo- and hyperthyroidism by different mechanisms","C. Amiodarone blocks TSH secretion from the pituitary","D. Amiodarone competitively binds T3 nuclear receptors causing hypothyroidism"]','D','Amiodarone contains ~37% iodine by weight. It causes type 1 AIT (iodine-induced excess synthesis in abnormal thyroid), type 2 AIT (destructive thyroiditis releasing pre-formed hormone), or hypothyroidism (Wolff-Chaikoff effect). It also inhibits type 1 deiodinase (raises T4, lowers T3) and T3 nuclear receptor binding. TSH is the key monitoring parameter.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','easy','mcq','Which clinical finding indicates levothyroxine over-replacement (iatrogenic thyrotoxicosis)?','["A. Bradycardia and weight gain","B. Constipation and dry skin","C. Atrial fibrillation, palpitations, and suppressed TSH","D. Hyponatraemia and hypoglycaemia"]','A','Over-replacement with levothyroxine causes iatrogenic thyrotoxicosis: palpitations, atrial fibrillation, tremor, weight loss, heat intolerance, anxiety, and insomnia. Chronically suppressed TSH causes osteoporosis through increased osteoclastic bone resorption. TSH below the lower normal limit confirms over-treatment; dose reduction is required.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Thyroid Drugs','medium','mcq','In subclinical hypothyroidism, levothyroxine treatment is most strongly indicated when:','["A. TSH is mildly elevated at 5-6 mIU/L with no symptoms in an elderly patient","B. TSH exceeds 10 mIU/L, or TSH 5-10 with positive TPO antibodies, symptoms, or pregnancy","C. A single TSH measurement is elevated on routine screening","D. The patient is elderly over 75 with atrial fibrillation"]','B','Current guidelines recommend levothyroxine treatment when TSH >10 mIU/L (risk of progression to overt hypothyroidism), or when TSH 5-10 mIU/L with positive anti-TPO antibodies (indicating Hashimotos thyroiditis and higher progression risk), symptoms attributable to hypothyroidism, or during pregnancy. A single elevated TSH should be confirmed with repeat testing before treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','easy','mcq','Hydrocortisone replacement in Addisons disease uses a split dose. Why is the larger dose given in the morning?','["A. To mimic the diurnal cortisol pattern which peaks in early morning around 8 AM","B. Evening cortisol causes insomnia if given late","C. Morning doses have better bioavailability","D. To prevent ACTH oversuppression at night"]','A','Physiological cortisol secretion follows a circadian rhythm, peaking at 6-8 AM and reaching nadir around midnight. Hydrocortisone replacement mimics this with a larger morning dose (typically two-thirds of daily dose) and smaller afternoon dose (one-third), sometimes split further. This improves energy, mood, and wellbeing compared to equal split dosing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','medium','mcq','Fludrocortisone is used in primary adrenal insufficiency. What is its main pharmacological function?','["A. Glucocorticoid replacement to suppress inflammation","B. Mineralocorticoid replacement - promotes renal sodium retention, potassium excretion, and water retention","C. ACTH analogue to stimulate adrenal cortex","D. Aldosterone antagonist to prevent hypertension"]','B','Fludrocortisone is a potent synthetic mineralocorticoid that acts on mineralocorticoid receptors in the renal distal tubule and collecting duct, promoting sodium and water retention and potassium excretion. It replaces aldosterone in Addisons disease and in salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia (21-hydroxylase deficiency).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','medium','mcq','Metyrapone is used to diagnose and treat Cushings syndrome. Its primary mechanism is:','["A. Blocking ACTH receptors on the adrenal cortex","B. Inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1), preventing conversion of 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol","C. Acting as a partial glucocorticoid receptor agonist to reduce cortisol effect","D. Enhancing hepatic clearance and conjugation of cortisol"]','C','Metyrapone inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1), the enzyme converting 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol in the adrenal cortex. This reduces cortisol production, causing compensatory ACTH rise and 11-deoxycortisol accumulation. The metyrapone test assesses HPA axis integrity. In Cushings syndrome, it reduces cortisol excess pending definitive surgery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','hard','mcq','Mitotane (o,p-DDD) is used in adrenocortical carcinoma. Which best describes its mechanism?','["A. Inhibiting ACTH secretion from the pituitary corticotrophs","B. Adrenolytic agent that destroys adrenocortical cells and inhibits multiple steroidogenic CYP enzymes","C. VEGF inhibitor reducing tumour vascularity","D. Selective glucocorticoid receptor antagonist"]','D','Mitotane is derived from the insecticide DDT and is adrenolytic: it selectively destroys adrenocortical cells (particularly zona fasciculata and reticularis) through reactive metabolites and inhibits CYP11A1, CYP11B1, CYP11B2 enzymes. Used as adjuvant therapy post-adrenalectomy for adrenocortical carcinoma. Glucocorticoid replacement is required as it destroys functioning adrenal tissue.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','easy','mcq','A patient on long-term prednisolone requires emergency abdominal surgery. What steroid management is needed?','["A. Double the oral prednisolone dose on the day of surgery only","B. Administer hydrocortisone stress dosing: 100 mg IV stat then 50 mg every 6-8 hours perioperatively","C. Stop prednisolone 24 hours before surgery to avoid wound healing impairment","D. Give synthetic ACTH (tetracosactide) to stimulate adrenal function acutely"]','A','Patients on long-term corticosteroids have HPA axis suppression and cannot mount a sufficient cortisol stress response (required cortisol rises 5-10-fold during major surgery). IV hydrocortisone stress dosing (100 mg stat, 50 mg every 6-8 hours) is given perioperatively to prevent Addisonian crisis (hypotension, hypoglycaemia, cardiovascular collapse).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','medium','mcq','Ketoconazole reduces cortisol excess in Cushings syndrome by:','["A. Blocking ACTH receptors on the adrenal gland","B. Inhibiting multiple cytochrome P450 steroidogenic enzymes (CYP11A1, CYP11B1, CYP17A1)","C. Enhancing cortisol binding to cortisol-binding globulin (CBG)","D. Stimulating adrenal cortex involution via anti-VEGF action"]','B','Ketoconazole is an imidazole antifungal that inhibits multiple CYP steroidogenic enzymes including CYP11A1 (cholesterol side-chain cleavage), CYP11B1 (11-beta-hydroxylase), and CYP17A1 (17-alpha-hydroxylase), reducing production of cortisol, androgens, and aldosterone. Used as second-line for Cushings syndrome. Monitoring LFTs essential due to hepatotoxicity risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','medium','mcq','In 21-hydroxylase deficiency (most common CAH), glucocorticoid replacement with hydrocortisone serves which dual purpose?','["A. Replaces deficient cortisol AND suppresses elevated ACTH, reducing excess androgen production","B. Replaces cortisol AND stimulates mineralocorticoid production","C. Blocks androgen receptors while replacing cortisol","D. Suppresses CRH AND replaces aldosterone"]','C','In 21-hydroxylase deficiency, cortisol synthesis is blocked, causing ACTH to rise (loss of negative feedback), which drives excess androgen production (androstenedione, testosterone). Hydrocortisone replacement restores cortisol levels AND provides negative feedback to suppress excess ACTH, thereby reducing androgen overproduction. Fludrocortisone is added separately for mineralocorticoid replacement in salt-wasting forms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','hard','mcq','Pasireotide (Signifor) is used in Cushings disease. Its therapeutic target is:','["A. Somatostatin receptors (predominantly SSTR5) on pituitary corticotroph adenoma cells, reducing ACTH secretion","B. Adrenal glucocorticoid synthesis enzymes","C. Hypothalamic CRH receptors","D. Pituitary TSH-secreting thyrotroph cells"]','D','Pasireotide is a multi-receptor somatostatin analogue with highest affinity for SSTR5 (also SSTR1, 2, 3). SSTR5 is predominantly expressed on corticotroph adenoma cells in Cushings disease. Binding SSTR5 inhibits ACTH secretion and reduces cortisol production. Key side effect: hyperglycaemia in ~73% of patients due to suppression of insulin and GLP-1 secretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','easy','mcq','What is the primary risk of abrupt cessation of long-term corticosteroid therapy?','["A. Rebound hypercortisolism causing Cushings syndrome","B. Acute adrenal insufficiency (Addisonian crisis) due to HPA axis suppression","C. Severe rebound hyperglycaemia","D. Sudden cytokine storm due to immune reactivation"]','A','Long-term exogenous glucocorticoids suppress the HPA axis via negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary. The adrenal glands atrophy. Abrupt withdrawal removes exogenous cortisol without the adrenal glands being able to immediately compensate, causing acute adrenal insufficiency (Addisonian crisis) with hypotension, hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, and hyperkalaemia. Gradual dose tapering is mandatory.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Adrenal Drugs','medium','mcq','Mifepristone (Korlym) is used in Cushings syndrome. Why cannot serum cortisol levels be used to monitor treatment response?','["A. Mifepristone suppresses cortisol secretion","B. Mifepristone blocks glucocorticoid receptors, causing compensatory ACTH rise and elevated cortisol levels despite clinical improvement","C. Cortisol assays are inaccurate in the presence of mifepristone","D. Mifepristone only reduces ACTH, not cortisol"]','B','Mifepristone is a glucocorticoid receptor antagonist. By blocking GRs, it removes cortisol negative feedback, causing ACTH to rise, which stimulates more cortisol secretion. Thus, serum cortisol (and ACTH) levels actually increase during effective treatment, making them useless as monitoring parameters. Clinical response (glucose, blood pressure, symptoms) guides dose adjustment instead.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','easy','mcq','Alendronate is a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate used in osteoporosis. Its mechanism is:','["A. Inhibiting osteoclast farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (mevalonate pathway), impairing osteoclast function and promoting apoptosis","B. Stimulating osteoblast bone formation via PTH receptor","C. Increasing calcium and phosphate absorption in the gut","D. Stimulating calcitonin secretion from thyroid C-cells"]','A','Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronic acid) are taken up by osteoclasts and inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway. This prevents prenylation of signalling proteins (Ras, Rho, Rac), impairing osteoclast cytoskeletal function, reducing ruffled border formation, and promoting osteoclast apoptosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','medium','mcq','A patient taking weekly alendronate develops severe epigastric pain. Which counselling instruction reduces this side effect?','["A. Take with food and milk to buffer the tablet","B. Take on an empty stomach with 200 mL plain water and remain upright for 30 minutes","C. Take at bedtime to avoid daytime symptoms","D. Crush the tablet and dissolve in orange juice for easier swallowing"]','B','Bisphosphonates cause oesophageal irritation and ulceration if they lodge in the oesophagus. To minimise this: take on a completely empty stomach with at least 200 mL plain water (not juice, coffee, or milk which impair absorption), remain sitting or standing upright for 30 minutes, and do not eat/drink anything else for 30-60 minutes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','medium','mcq','Teriparatide (recombinant PTH 1-34) differs fundamentally from bisphosphonates because it:','["A. Inhibits osteoclastic bone resorption","B. Is an anabolic agent that stimulates new bone formation via osteoblast PTH-1 receptor activation","C. Is given orally once weekly","D. Acts as a calcium supplement increasing bone mineral content directly"]','C','Teriparatide, when given intermittently (daily subcutaneous injection), activates PTH-1 receptors on osteoblasts and their precursors, stimulating new bone formation (anabolic effect). This contrasts with all other anti-osteoporosis drugs that work by reducing bone resorption. Duration limited to 24 months due to osteosarcoma risk in animal studies (not confirmed in humans).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','hard','mcq','Cinacalcet treats secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD by which mechanism?','["A. Increasing intestinal calcium absorption to suppress PTH","B. Acting as a calcimimetic: positive allosteric modulator of calcium-sensing receptors (CaSR) on parathyroid cells, reducing PTH secretion","C. Inhibiting 1-alpha-hydroxylase to reduce calcitriol","D. Blocking vitamin D receptors in parathyroid gland cells"]','D','Cinacalcet is a calcimimetic that acts as a positive allosteric modulator of CaSR on parathyroid chief cells, increasing receptor sensitivity to extracellular calcium. This suppresses PTH secretion without raising serum calcium (unlike vitamin D analogues which cause hypercalcaemia). Used in secondary HPT in dialysis patients and in parathyroid carcinoma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','easy','mcq','Which vitamin D formulation is required in patients with advanced CKD (stage 4-5) who cannot perform the final 1-alpha-hydroxylation step?','["A. Cholecalciferol (vitamin D3)","B. Ergocalciferol (vitamin D2)","C. Calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3) or alfacalcidol (1-alpha-hydroxyvitamin D3)","D. Calcidiol (25-hydroxyvitamin D3)"]','A','CKD stages 4-5 have markedly reduced renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity and cannot convert 25-OH vitamin D to active calcitriol. Calcitriol or alfacalcidol (which is converted to calcitriol by 25-hydroxylation in the liver) must be prescribed. Standard cholecalciferol supplementation is insufficient as it requires the impaired renal hydroxylation step.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','medium','mcq','Denosumab (Prolia) differs from bisphosphonates by working via:','["A. Inhibiting mevalonate pathway in osteoclasts","B. Binding RANKL, preventing it from activating RANK on osteoclast precursors and mature osteoclasts","C. Acting as a PTH analogue to simultaneously stimulate osteoblasts","D. Chelating calcium ions to prevent bone crystal growth"]','B','Denosumab is a human monoclonal antibody targeting RANKL (receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa-B ligand). By binding RANKL, it prevents osteoclast formation, function, and survival. Unlike bisphosphonates, it does not incorporate into bone matrix; its effect is fully reversible on discontinuation (bone turnover rebounds), requiring transition to another agent if stopped.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','medium','mcq','Which rare but serious adverse effects are associated with long-term bisphosphonate use (>5 years)?','["A. Hypercalcaemia and hypervitaminosis D","B. Atypical subtrochanteric femoral fractures and osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ)","C. Renal calcium stone formation and hypercalciuria","D. Hyperphosphataemia and ectopic calcification"]','C','Long-term bisphosphonate therapy (>5 years) is associated with: (1) atypical femoral fractures (due to over-suppression of bone remodelling, preventing repair of microdamage) and (2) osteonecrosis of the jaw (particularly with IV bisphosphonates in oncology patients). A bisphosphonate drug holiday after 3-5 years is recommended in low-moderate risk patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','hard','mcq','Romosozumab (Evenity) is a novel bone anabolic agent. Its mechanism involves:','["A. Inhibiting sclerostin, an osteocyte-derived Wnt signalling inhibitor, promoting osteoblast activity and bone formation","B. Stimulating PTH-1 receptors continuously to drive osteoblast formation","C. Binding RANKL to prevent bone resorption simultaneously","D. Activating osteoclast apoptosis via caspase-3 pathway activation"]','D','Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody that inhibits sclerostin, a protein secreted by osteocytes that normally suppresses Wnt/beta-catenin signalling in osteoblasts. Inhibiting sclerostin promotes osteoblast differentiation and bone formation (anabolic effect) while also reducing bone resorption. However, it carries a cardiovascular warning following increased MI/stroke risk in ARCH trial.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','easy','mcq','Why must calcium and vitamin D supplementation accompany bisphosphonate therapy?','["A. To prevent bisphosphonate-induced hypocalcaemia from reduced osteoclastic bone calcium release","B. To enhance bisphosphonate gastrointestinal absorption","C. To reduce GI side effects by chelating bisphosphonate in the gut","D. To improve renal clearance and reduce nephrotoxicity"]','A','Bisphosphonates inhibit osteoclastic bone resorption, reducing the calcium released from bone into circulation. This can cause hypocalcaemia, which can be symptomatic and potentially dangerous (tetany, arrhythmias). Adequate calcium (1000-1500 mg/day dietary + supplemental) and vitamin D (800-2000 IU/day) are essential co-prescriptions with all bisphosphonates.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Calcium & Bone Drugs','medium','mcq','Strontium ranelate was withdrawn from widespread clinical use primarily due to risk of:','["A. Severe osteonecrosis of the jaw with prolonged use","B. Increased risk of serious cardiovascular events including MI and stroke","C. Renal impairment and nephrolithiasis","D. Hypocalcaemia and secondary hyperparathyroidism"]','B','Strontium ranelate (dual mechanism: anti-resorptive and mildly anabolic) was restricted then largely withdrawn following findings of significantly increased risk of serious cardiovascular events (MI, stroke) and venous thromboembolism in post-marketing surveillance and meta-analyses. EMA restricted it to severe osteoporosis when other therapies cannot be used, with strict cardiovascular contraindication screening.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','easy','mcq','Cabergoline is first-line treatment for prolactinoma. Its mechanism is:','["A. Dopamine D2 receptor agonist that suppresses prolactin secretion and causes tumour shrinkage","B. Oestrogen receptor antagonist reducing prolactin synthesis","C. Somatostatin analogue inhibiting GH and prolactin","D. GnRH antagonist reducing pituitary stimulation"]','A','Cabergoline is a long-acting dopamine D2 receptor agonist. Dopamine (from the hypothalamus) normally inhibits prolactin secretion from lactotroph cells. Cabergoline activates D2 receptors directly, suppressing prolactin production and secretion. It also causes tumour shrinkage in prolactinomas in >80% of cases. Twice-weekly dosing improves compliance over older bromocriptine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','medium','mcq','Octreotide treats acromegaly by which mechanism?','["A. Blocking GH receptors in peripheral tissues, reducing IGF-1 signalling","B. Somatostatin receptor agonist (SSTR2/5) inhibiting GH secretion from pituitary somatotroph cells","C. Stimulating hypothalamic somatostatin production","D. Competitive IGF-1 receptor antagonist in the liver"]','B','Octreotide is a synthetic somatostatin analogue that binds SSTR2 and SSTR5 on GH-secreting pituitary somatotroph cells, inhibiting GH secretion. Reduced GH lowers hepatic IGF-1 production. Octreotide requires thrice-daily SC injections; long-acting release (Sandostatin LAR) is given monthly IM. Gallstones are a common side effect due to reduced gallbladder motility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','medium','mcq','Desmopressin (DDAVP) treats central diabetes insipidus by acting primarily on:','["A. Vasopressin V1a receptors causing vasoconstriction","B. Vasopressin V2 receptors in renal collecting ducts, inserting aquaporin-2 water channels","C. Oxytocin receptors in the renal tubule","D. Aldosterone receptors in the distal nephron"]','C','Desmopressin is a selective V2 receptor agonist (minimal V1 activity). V2 receptor activation on renal collecting duct principal cells increases cAMP, activating PKA, which phosphorylates aquaporin-2 water channels, causing their insertion into the apical membrane. This enables water reabsorption down the osmotic gradient, concentrating urine and reducing polyuria.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','hard','mcq','Pegvisomant treats acromegaly refractory to somatostatin analogues. Its mechanism is:','["A. Higher-affinity somatostatin analogue with greater SSTR2 binding","B. GH receptor antagonist blocking JAK2-STAT5 signalling and reducing IGF-1","C. Dopamine agonist suppressing GH secretion from adenoma cells","D. Anti-GH monoclonal antibody neutralising circulating GH directly"]','D','Pegvisomant is a recombinant GH analogue (with 9 amino acid mutations) that competitively binds GH receptors but does not activate them, blocking GH signalling. This prevents GH-induced JAK2/STAT5 activation and IGF-1 production in the liver. IGF-1 normalises in >90% of patients. GH levels rise compensatorily (not used for monitoring). IGF-1 is the efficacy biomarker.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','easy','mcq','Somatropin (recombinant GH) treatment response is best monitored by measuring:','["A. Serum cortisol levels","B. IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1) levels, adjusted for age and sex","C. Serum prolactin","D. Urinary catecholamines"]','A','IGF-1 is the primary monitoring parameter for somatropin. GH stimulates hepatic IGF-1 synthesis; IGF-1 reflects integrated GH action. Unlike pulsatile GH, IGF-1 levels are stable throughout the day, making them practical for monitoring. Dose is adjusted to maintain age- and sex-adjusted IGF-1 within the normal range. Fasting glucose also requires monitoring.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','medium','mcq','When GnRH agonists (e.g., leuprorelin) are given continuously, what paradoxical effect occurs?','["A. Continuous GnRH agonism continuously stimulates LH and FSH","B. Continuous exposure causes pituitary GnRH receptor downregulation and desensitisation, paradoxically suppressing LH, FSH, and sex hormones","C. Continuous GnRH only suppresses LH without affecting FSH","D. Continuous GnRH causes hyperprolactinaemia via D2 receptor blockade"]','B','GnRH receptors require pulsatile stimulation to maintain sensitivity. Continuous GnRH (or long-acting GnRH agonists) causes receptor downregulation and uncoupling from signal transduction, leading to paradoxical suppression of LH, FSH, testosterone, and oestrogen after an initial flare. This principle is exploited therapeutically in prostate cancer, endometriosis, precocious puberty, and IVF downregulation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','medium','mcq','Lanreotide Autogel differs from standard octreotide in which important way?','["A. Has shorter duration requiring daily injection","B. Is formulated as a deep subcutaneous depot injection given every 4 weeks","C. Has selective SSTR2 activity only, unlike octreotide which has SSTR2/5","D. Cannot be used post-operatively for residual GH excess"]','B','Lanreotide Autogel (Somatuline Depot) is formulated to form a viscous gel on injection that slowly releases lanreotide, providing 4-weekly dosing (can be extended to every 6-8 weeks in well-controlled patients). This significantly improves adherence compared to octreotide SC three times daily. Both have similar SSTR2/5 selectivity and comparable efficacy in acromegaly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','hard','mcq','Mecasermin (recombinant IGF-1) treats primary IGF-1 deficiency (Laron syndrome). This condition is characterised by:','["A. Low GH levels with low IGF-1","B. Normal to elevated GH levels with low IGF-1 due to GH receptor mutations","C. Low GH but paradoxically normal IGF-1","D. Pituitary GH-secreting macroadenoma causing GH excess"]','D','Laron syndrome (growth hormone insensitivity syndrome) is caused by GH receptor mutations or post-receptor signalling defects. GH is secreted normally or excessively (due to loss of IGF-1 negative feedback), but cannot signal via its receptor, resulting in very low IGF-1 despite high GH levels. Mecasermin (recombinant IGF-1) replaces the deficient downstream mediator. Hypoglycaemia is a key dose-limiting adverse effect.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','easy','mcq','Bromocriptine compared to cabergoline for prolactinoma treatment:','["A. Has longer half-life allowing once-weekly dosing","B. Has more side effects and requires daily dosing, making cabergoline the preferred first-line agent","C. Is more effective at normalising prolactin levels","D. Has superior evidence for tumour volume reduction"]','A','Bromocriptine is an older ergot-derived dopamine agonist requiring daily to three-times-daily dosing and commonly causing nausea, vomiting, postural hypotension, and nasal congestion. Cabergoline has a longer half-life (65 hours), is dosed once or twice weekly, is better tolerated, achieves prolactin normalisation in ~80% vs ~70% with bromocriptine, and is more effective for tumour shrinkage. Cabergoline is the preferred first-line treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Pituitary Drugs','medium','mcq','Tolvaptan (Samsca), a vasopressin V2 receptor antagonist, is used in:','["A. Central diabetes insipidus to concentrate urine by sensitising V2 receptors","B. SIADH - chronic hyponatraemia - to correct sodium by promoting selective free water excretion (aquaresis)","C. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus to bypass ADH resistance","D. Acromegaly as second-line after somatostatin analogues"]','B','Tolvaptan blocks V2 receptors in the renal collecting duct, preventing ADH-stimulated aquaporin-2 insertion. This results in aquaresis (electrolyte-sparing free water excretion), raising serum sodium in SIADH. Correction must not exceed 8-10 mmol/L per 24 hours to avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome. Liver toxicity (FDA warning) limits long-term use in SIADH.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','easy','mcq','Glucocorticoids exert anti-inflammatory effects primarily through which molecular mechanism?','["A. Binding cytosolic glucocorticoid receptors that translocate to the nucleus, inducing lipocortin-1 to inhibit phospholipase A2 and reducing arachidonic acid release","B. Directly inhibiting COX-1 and COX-2 cyclooxygenase enzymes","C. Directly neutralising TNF-alpha and IL-6 cytokines","D. Inhibiting 5-lipoxygenase to prevent leukotriene synthesis"]','A','Glucocorticoids bind intracellular GRs, which dimerize and translocate to the nucleus to activate transcription of lipocortin-1 (annexin A1) and suppress NF-kB-driven pro-inflammatory gene transcription. Lipocortin-1 inhibits phospholipase A2, reducing arachidonic acid liberation from membrane phospholipids, thereby reducing both prostaglandins and leukotrienes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','medium','mcq','Which corticosteroid has the highest mineralocorticoid potency relative to glucocorticoid activity?','["A. Dexamethasone","B. Prednisolone","C. Fludrocortisone","D. Triamcinolone"]','C','Fludrocortisone has the highest mineralocorticoid potency of clinically used steroids (~125x hydrocortisone for mineralocorticoid activity) with modest glucocorticoid activity (~10x hydrocortisone). Dexamethasone has virtually no mineralocorticoid activity (making it ideal when fluid retention is undesirable). Fludrocortisone is used exclusively as mineralocorticoid replacement in Addisons disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','medium','mcq','Long-term systemic corticosteroid therapy causes osteoporosis by which combination of mechanisms?','["A. Reducing intestinal calcium absorption, increasing renal calcium excretion, and directly suppressing osteoblast activity","B. Increasing PTH secretion and stimulating osteoclast RANKL only","C. Reducing vitamin D skin synthesis","D. Increasing bone turnover through osteoblast overstimulation followed by exhaustion"]','B','Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis occurs through: (1) reduced intestinal calcium absorption (decreased vitamin D receptor expression), (2) increased renal calcium excretion, (3) direct suppression of osteoblast differentiation and function, and (4) indirect effects via sex hormone suppression and secondary hyperparathyroidism. Bisphosphonate prophylaxis is recommended for patients taking >7.5 mg/day prednisolone for >3 months.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','hard','mcq','Why do inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) have significantly fewer systemic side effects than equivalent anti-inflammatory oral doses?','["A. ICS have no glucocorticoid receptor binding activity in the systemic circulation","B. Local delivery to airways achieves therapeutic effect, with extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism inactivating swallowed/absorbed fractions","C. ICS are not absorbed from the airways at all","D. ICS are rapidly excreted renally without metabolic activation"]','D','ICS deliver high drug concentrations locally at the airway with relatively low systemic absorption. Any portion swallowed (especially from large particle deposits) or systemically absorbed undergoes extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism (>90% for fluticasone propionate, ~80% for budesonide), dramatically reducing systemic bioavailability and side effects. High-dose ICS can still cause adrenal suppression, particularly in children.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','easy','mcq','Which metabolic complication is most important to monitor in patients starting high-dose systemic corticosteroids?','["A. Hyperglycaemia and steroid-induced diabetes mellitus","B. Hypoglycaemia from insulin stimulation","C. Metabolic acidosis with hypokalaemia","D. Rapid weight loss and catabolism"]','A','Glucocorticoids cause hyperglycaemia via: (1) stimulating hepatic gluconeogenesis, (2) inducing insulin resistance in muscle and adipose tissue, (3) promoting hepatic glycogenolysis, and (4) possibly reducing beta-cell insulin secretion at high doses. Blood glucose monitoring (particularly postprandial) is essential for all patients starting systemic corticosteroids, especially those at risk of diabetes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','medium','mcq','When is corticosteroid tapering mandatory rather than abrupt stopping?','["A. Only if the patient has been on steroids for more than 6 months","B. When taking doses equivalent to >5 mg prednisolone per day for more than 3-4 weeks","C. Only in patients with pre-existing diagnosed adrenal insufficiency","D. Tapering is never required as the adrenals recover independently within 48 hours"]','B','HPA axis suppression occurs with systemic corticosteroids equivalent to >5 mg prednisolone/day for more than 3-4 weeks. Abrupt withdrawal removes exogenous cortisol without adrenal recovery, precipitating acute adrenal insufficiency. Tapering allows gradual HPA axis recovery. Short courses (<3 weeks) can generally be stopped abruptly if prescribed for acute conditions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','medium','mcq','Which clinical features characterise exogenous Cushings syndrome from long-term oral corticosteroids?','["A. Central obesity with moon face, buffalo hump, purple striae, proximal myopathy, and hypertension","B. Weight loss, hyperpigmentation, and postural hypotension","C. Acromegaly features with hyperglycaemia and tall stature","D. Thin skin, alopecia, and adrenal gland enlargement on imaging"]','C','Exogenous glucocorticoid-induced Cushings syndrome features: central obesity (moon face, buffalo hump, supraclavicular fat pads), proximal myopathy, purple striae, easy bruising, thin skin, hypertension, hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, cataracts, and immunosuppression. Unlike endogenous Cushings disease, hyperpigmentation does NOT occur with exogenous steroids (it requires ACTH excess, which is suppressed by exogenous steroids).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','hard','mcq','Why is dexamethasone preferred over prednisolone for treating vasogenic brain oedema around tumours?','["A. Dexamethasone has lower anti-inflammatory potency and fewer mineralocorticoid effects","B. Dexamethasone has the highest anti-inflammatory potency (25-30x hydrocortisone) and negligible mineralocorticoid activity, preventing sodium retention","C. Dexamethasone crosses the blood-brain barrier less than prednisolone","D. Dexamethasone has shorter half-life allowing better titration"]','D','Dexamethasone is 25-30 times more potent than hydrocortisone as a glucocorticoid and has essentially no mineralocorticoid activity (no sodium/water retention to worsen cerebral oedema). Its high lipid solubility allows good CNS penetration. It stabilises blood-brain barrier endothelial tight junctions, reducing vasogenic oedema surrounding brain tumours. Its long half-life allows twice-daily dosing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','easy','mcq','Posterior subcapsular cataracts are a known complication of corticosteroid therapy. Which route of administration can cause them?','["A. Only intravenous high-dose pulse therapy","B. Systemic (oral or IV), topical ophthalmic, or prolonged high-dose inhaled corticosteroids","C. Topical skin preparations only","D. Only intraocular corticosteroid injections"]','A','Posterior subcapsular cataracts (PSC) are associated with systemic, topical ophthalmic, and even prolonged high-dose inhaled corticosteroid use. Children are particularly susceptible. PSC characteristically impairs vision in bright light and reading. Annual ophthalmological examination is recommended for patients on long-term corticosteroids regardless of route.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Corticosteroid Pharmacology','medium','mcq','A patient taking 32 mg methylprednisolone daily switches to prednisolone. What equivalent prednisolone dose is correct?','["A. 25 mg","B. 40 mg","C. 50 mg","D. 64 mg"]','B','Corticosteroid equivalence (anti-inflammatory potency relative to hydrocortisone): hydrocortisone 1 (20 mg), prednisolone 4 (5 mg), methylprednisolone 5 (4 mg), dexamethasone 25-30 (0.75 mg). Conversion: methylprednisolone 4 mg = prednisolone 5 mg, therefore 32 mg methylprednisolone = 32 × (5/4) = 40 mg prednisolone. This conversion is widely used clinically when switching between steroids.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','easy','mcq','Combined oral contraceptive pills (COCPs) prevent pregnancy primarily by:','["A. Inhibiting the hypothalamic-pituitary axis: progestogen suppresses LH surge (preventing ovulation) and oestrogen suppresses FSH","B. Thickening cervical mucus alone","C. Preventing implantation of a fertilised ovum as the primary mechanism","D. Blocking oestrogen receptors in the endometrium"]','A','COCPs prevent pregnancy by multiple mechanisms: (1) progestogen suppresses LH surge (primary mechanism, preventing ovulation), (2) oestrogen suppresses FSH and prevents follicle selection, (3) progestogen thickens cervical mucus impeding sperm, and (4) altered endometrial lining. The combined pill is >99% effective when taken correctly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','medium','mcq','Oestrogen-only HRT (without progestogen) significantly increases risk of which malignancy?','["A. Ovarian cancer only","B. Endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma due to unopposed oestrogen stimulation of endometrial proliferation","C. Breast cancer only","D. Cervical cancer"]','B','Unopposed oestrogen stimulates endometrial cell proliferation (mitosis), leading to hyperplasia and carcinoma over time. Combined HRT (oestrogen + progestogen) prevents this by inducing regular endometrial shedding. Oestrogen-only HRT is therefore appropriate only in women who have had a hysterectomy. Risk increases with duration of use.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','medium','mcq','Raloxifene (a SERM) acts as an oestrogen agonist in bone but antagonist in breast and uterus. This tissue selectivity is due to:','["A. Different oestrogen receptor subtypes (ER-alpha vs ER-beta) expressed in different tissues and different co-regulatory protein recruitment","B. Differential tissue drug metabolism converting it to active metabolites","C. Inability to enter breast tissue due to molecular size","D. Competitive antagonism only when oestrogen levels are high"]','C','SERMs achieve tissue selectivity through differential ER subtype expression (ER-alpha vs ER-beta ratios vary by tissue) and by recruiting different co-activator and co-repressor proteins upon receptor binding. The conformational change induced by raloxifene vs oestradiol differs, leading to agonist or antagonist activity depending on the transcriptional environment of each tissue type.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','hard','mcq','Testosterone replacement in hypogonadal men is absolutely contraindicated in:','["A. Type 2 diabetes mellitus","B. Prostate cancer or significant elevation of PSA suggesting occult prostate cancer","C. Metabolic syndrome without comorbidities","D. Mild-to-moderate osteoporosis"]','D','Testosterone replacement is absolutely contraindicated in prostate cancer (androgenic stimulation promotes tumour growth and metastasis) and in men with significantly elevated PSA suspicious for occult prostate cancer. Other important contraindications include polycythaemia, severe untreated sleep apnoea, severe lower urinary tract symptoms (IPSS >19), and desire for fertility (testosterone suppresses spermatogenesis via GnRH/LH suppression).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','easy','mcq','Which serious adverse effect is most associated with the combined oral contraceptive pill?','["A. Venous thromboembolism (VTE)","B. Severe hypoglycaemia","C. Hypothyroidism","D. Adrenal insufficiency"]','A','Combined OCP increases VTE risk 3-4-fold through oestrogen-mediated hepatic synthesis of coagulation factors (II, VII, VIII, X fibrinogen increases; protein S decreases). Third-generation progestogens (desogestrel, gestodene) carry higher VTE risk than second-generation (levonorgestrel). Absolute VTE risk remains low in healthy young women (~3-4 per 10,000 women-years vs 1-2 in non-users).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','medium','mcq','The WHI trial linked combined HRT (CEE + MPA) to increased risk of which cancer, contributing to a significant reduction in HRT prescribing?','["A. Ovarian cancer","B. Breast cancer","C. Endometrial cancer","D. Cervical cancer"]','B','The Womens Health Initiative (2002) found combined HRT with conjugated equine oestrogens plus medroxyprogesterone acetate increased breast cancer risk (HR ~1.26) after mean 5.2 years of use. This finding triggered a sharp decline in HRT prescribing. Re-analysis suggests younger, recently menopausal women have a more favourable benefit-risk ratio. Micronised progesterone may carry lower breast cancer risk than MPA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','medium','mcq','Why does transdermal oestrogen in HRT carry a lower VTE risk than oral oestrogen?','["A. Transdermal oestrogen bypasses first-pass hepatic metabolism, avoiding stimulation of hepatic coagulation factor synthesis","B. Transdermal oestrogen has lower bioavailability and weaker systemic effects","C. Transdermal oestrogen has stronger bone-protective effects","D. Transdermal oestrogen prevents hot flushes more effectively"]','C','Oral oestrogens undergo first-pass hepatic metabolism, stimulating hepatic synthesis of coagulation factors (fibrinogen, factors VII, VIII, X) and reducing anticoagulant proteins (protein S, antithrombin). This explains oral HRTs approximately 2-fold increased VTE risk. Transdermal oestrogen bypasses hepatic first-pass, maintaining physiological oestradiol:oestrone ratio and not significantly affecting clotting factors.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','hard','mcq','Tibolone is used for postmenopausal symptoms. Which best characterises its pharmacological activity?','["A. Pure oestrogen receptor agonist in all tissues","B. A prodrug metabolised to metabolites with tissue-specific oestrogenic, progestogenic, and androgenic activity","C. A pure SERM with agonist activity only in bone","D. A GnRH agonist reducing gonadal hormone production"]','D','Tibolone is a synthetic steroid prodrug. It is metabolised to: (1) 3-alpha and 3-beta hydroxy metabolites (oestrogenic - relieve vasomotor symptoms, prevent bone loss, improve mood and libido), and (2) delta-4 isomer (progestogenic and androgenic - suppresses endometrial proliferation, androgenic effects on wellbeing and libido). Its tissue-selectivity means it does not stimulate breast tissue as much as conventional combined HRT.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','easy','mcq','Testosterone replacement in transgender men (female-to-male) primarily achieves which effects?','["A. Voice deepening, facial and body hair growth, clitoral enlargement, and cessation of menses","B. Breast development and hip widening","C. Increased body fat with female distribution","D. Increased FSH and LH to stimulate ovulation"]','A','Testosterone in female-to-male gender-affirming therapy produces masculinising effects: voice deepening (irreversible), clitoral growth, facial and body hair increase, male-pattern baldness (in predisposed individuals), muscle mass increase with fat redistribution, cessation of menstruation, and improved gender dysphoria. Routes include IM injections (enanthate/cypionate) or transdermal gel.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Sex Hormones & HRT','medium','mcq','Clomifene citrate is used for anovulatory infertility. It increases ovulation by:','["A. Stimulating the pituitary with GnRH-like activity","B. Blocking hypothalamic oestrogen receptors, removing negative feedback and increasing endogenous FSH and LH secretion","C. Directly stimulating ovarian follicles via FSH analogue activity","D. Acting as a progesterone antagonist to trigger follicle rupture"]','B','Clomifene is a SERM acting as an oestrogen receptor antagonist in the hypothalamus. By blocking ER, it prevents circulating oestrogens from providing negative feedback to the hypothalamus and pituitary, increasing GnRH pulse frequency and FSH/LH secretion. This stimulates ovarian folliculogenesis and ovulation. Multiple pregnancy risk ~8% (mostly twins). Visual disturbances (blurred vision) are an indication to stop treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','easy','mcq','Orlistat reduces body weight by which mechanism?','["A. Inhibiting gastric and pancreatic lipase, reducing dietary fat absorption by approximately 30%","B. Central appetite suppression via serotonin and norepinephrine pathways","C. Activating brown adipose tissue thermogenesis","D. Blocking ghrelin receptors in the hypothalamus to reduce hunger"]','A','Orlistat is a gastric and pancreatic lipase inhibitor. By binding irreversibly to the active site of these enzymes in the GI lumen, it prevents hydrolysis of dietary triglycerides into absorbable fatty acids and monoglycerides, reducing fat absorption by ~30%. Unabsorbed fat passes into the colon causing steatorrhoea, oily spotting, and faecal urgency. Fat-soluble vitamin supplementation is essential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','medium','mcq','Semaglutide 2.4 mg (Wegovy) achieves greater weight loss than older obesity drugs. Which mechanisms explain this?','["A. Increasing basal metabolic rate by 20% and activating brown fat","B. Central appetite suppression via hypothalamic GLP-1 receptors, slowed gastric emptying, and reduced food reward signalling","C. Blocking intestinal fat absorption similar to orlistat","D. Increasing glycosuria to excrete excess calories renally"]','B','High-dose semaglutide activates GLP-1 receptors in the hypothalamic arcuate and paraventricular nuclei, reducing appetite and food intake, and in the mesolimbic system, reducing food reward. Gastric emptying is slowed, reducing meal size tolerance. The STEP 1 trial showed 14.9% mean weight loss vs 2.4% placebo. The SELECT trial showed 20% reduction in MACE in non-diabetic CV disease patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','medium','mcq','Naltrexone/bupropion (Mysimba) treats obesity. Bupropions contribution to weight loss is:','["A. Opioid receptor antagonism blocking food reward","B. Activating hypothalamic POMC neurons, increasing alpha-MSH release, and promoting satiety via MC4 receptors","C. Inhibiting gastric lipase to reduce fat absorption","D. Blocking cannabinoid CB1 receptors to reduce appetite"]','C','Bupropion (dopamine/norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor) activates hypothalamic proopiomelanocortin (POMC) neurons, increasing alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH). Alpha-MSH acts on melanocortin-4 (MC4) receptors to reduce food intake and increase energy expenditure. Naltrexone blocks opioid auto-inhibitory feedback on POMC neurons, amplifying bupropions pro-satiety effect. Combined, they achieve ~5-8% weight loss.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','hard','mcq','Tirzepatide (Mounjaro) is a dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonist. In the SURMOUNT-1 trial (non-diabetic obesity), it achieved:','["A. Equivalent weight loss to semaglutide 2.4 mg (~15%)","B. Approximately 20.9% mean weight loss with the 15 mg dose at 72 weeks","C. Only modest weight loss of 9% due to GIP receptor-mediated insulin hypersecretion","D. Superior reduction in MACE events compared to semaglutide"]','D','SURMOUNT-1 (tirzepatide in non-diabetic obesity) showed mean weight loss of ~20.9% with 15 mg at 72 weeks, vs ~14.9% with semaglutide 2.4 mg (STEP 1). GIP receptor activation synergises with GLP-1 effects on appetite, energy expenditure, and insulin secretion. GIP also promotes adipose lipolysis. SURMOUNT-MMO is the ongoing CV outcomes trial.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','easy','mcq','Patients taking orlistat require which essential dietary and supplementation advice?','["A. Maintain fat intake below 30% of calories per meal and take a daily fat-soluble vitamin supplement (A, D, E, K)","B. Adopt a ketogenic diet to maximise fat loss","C. Take orlistat on an empty stomach to maximise lipase inhibition","D. Avoid carbohydrates to prevent GI side effects"]','A','Orlistats GI side effects (steatorrhoea, oily spotting, faecal urgency, flatulence) worsen significantly with high-fat meals as unabsorbed fat irritates the colon. Fat should not exceed 30% of caloric intake and be distributed equally across meals. Additionally, fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are incompletely absorbed; a multivitamin supplement taken 2 hours before or after orlistat is essential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','medium','mcq','Liraglutide 3.0 mg (Saxenda) differs from liraglutide 1.8 mg (Victoza) in that Saxenda is:','["A. A different molecular formulation with modified receptor selectivity","B. Licensed specifically for chronic weight management, not for glucose lowering in T2DM","C. Associated with significantly fewer GI side effects due to slower dose escalation","D. Contraindicated in patients with type 2 diabetes"]','B','Liraglutide 3.0 mg (Saxenda) is the identical molecule to liraglutide 1.8 mg (Victoza) but is licensed for chronic weight management in adults with BMI ≥30 kg/m2, or ≥27 kg/m2 with weight-related comorbidities. Higher dose produces greater appetite suppression and weight loss (~5-7%). GI side effects are more frequent at 3.0 mg; gradual dose escalation from 0.6 mg weekly minimises nausea.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','medium','mcq','Which key risk requires patient counselling when prescribing GLP-1 agonists for obesity?','["A. Hypothyroidism and thyroid nodule formation","B. Risk of acute pancreatitis and medullary thyroid carcinoma (black-box warning)","C. Severe hepatotoxicity requiring monthly LFT monitoring","D. Peripheral neuropathy and vitamin B12 deficiency"]','C','GLP-1 agonists carry: (1) FDA black-box warning for medullary thyroid carcinoma (contraindicated in personal/family history of MTC or MEN2) based on rodent data, and (2) risk of acute pancreatitis (reported in clinical trials; patients should report severe persistent abdominal pain). Stop if pancreatitis confirmed. Gallstones are also more common due to reduced gallbladder motility from rapid weight loss.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','hard','mcq','The SELECT trial (2023) evaluated semaglutide 2.4 mg in patients without diabetes who had obesity and established cardiovascular disease. What was the key finding?','["A. Semaglutide reduced HbA1c without cardiovascular benefit","B. Semaglutide reduced MACE (CV death, non-fatal MI, stroke) by 20% vs placebo over 5 years","C. Weight loss alone drove a 5% MACE reduction","D. Semaglutide increased HF hospitalisation in this population"]','D','The SELECT trial (N=17,604) showed semaglutide 2.4 mg reduced the primary composite endpoint (CV death, non-fatal MI, non-fatal stroke) by 20% (HR 0.80, p<0.001) versus placebo over 5 years in adults with obesity and established CVD but without diabetes. Mean weight loss was 9.4% vs 0.9% placebo. This established GLP-1 agonists as cardioprotective in non-diabetic obesity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','easy','mcq','Phentermine is an older short-term obesity treatment. Its class and mechanism are:','["A. Lipase inhibitor reducing dietary fat absorption","B. Sympathomimetic amine (amphetamine derivative) releasing norepinephrine and dopamine, suppressing appetite","C. GLP-1 receptor agonist with central satiety effects","D. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor causing appetite suppression"]','A','Phentermine is a Schedule IV sympathomimetic amine (phenylethylamine/amphetamine derivative) that releases norepinephrine and dopamine in hypothalamic satiety centres, suppressing appetite. It is approved only for short-term use (<12 weeks) due to risk of dependence, tachycardia, and hypertension. Phentermine/topiramate extended-release (Qsymia) combines mechanisms for longer-term use.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Endocrinology','Pharmacology','Drugs for Obesity','medium','mcq','The STEP 5 trial evaluated semaglutide 2.4 mg for obesity. Which finding from this long-term trial is most clinically significant?','["A. Semaglutide lost efficacy after 1 year","B. Weight loss of approximately 15% was maintained at 104 weeks (2 years), with regain occurring upon discontinuation","C. Semaglutide caused irreversible weight loss requiring no ongoing treatment","D. The drug was superior only in patients with type 2 diabetes"]','B','STEP 5 (104 weeks, 2-year duration) showed semaglutide 2.4 mg maintained ~15.2% mean weight loss at 2 years vs 2.6% placebo. This confirmed durability of effect with continued treatment. Importantly, when semaglutide is discontinued (STEP 4 withdrawal sub-study), patients regain approximately two-thirds of lost weight within 1 year, emphasising that obesity is a chronic condition requiring ongoing treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
